---
import BaseLayout from "../../site/layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import "../../site/styles/comparison-control-study.css";
---

<BaseLayout title="Comparison control study — Mourne Made" description="A visual study of the before-and-after comparison control." hideFooter>
  <main class="comparison-control-study">
    <div class="shell comparison-control-study-intro">
      <a class="comparison-control-study-back" href="/prototypes/arrow-study/">← Arrow study</a>
      <p class="home-kicker">Mourne Made · component study</p>
      <h1>How should the before-and-after invite a drag?</h1>
      <p class="comparison-control-study-lede">Three handles for the main homepage interaction. The control needs to be obvious without sitting on top of the work.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="shell comparison-control-study-grid">
      <article class="comparison-option"><p class="comparison-option-name"><span>A</span> Solid handle</p><div class="comparison-demo"><div class="comparison-line"></div><div class="comparison-handle comparison-handle--solid">↔</div></div><p>Direct and highly visible. The handle reads as an object you can grab.</p></article>
      <article class="comparison-option"><p class="comparison-option-name"><span>B</span> Blue handle</p><div class="comparison-demo"><div class="comparison-line"></div><div class="comparison-handle comparison-handle--blue">↔</div></div><p>Uses the primary brand colour and stays legible against varied imagery.</p></article>
      <article class="comparison-option"><p class="comparison-option-name"><span>C</span> Rule only</p><div class="comparison-demo"><div class="comparison-line"></div><div class="comparison-handle comparison-handle--rule"><span></span></div></div><p>Most minimal. The vertical rule carries the interaction; the drag cue stays quiet.</p></article>
    </div>
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
